Thus has the infinitely wise God so contrived the order of things and the constitution of man, that communicating happiness to others is a likely way to procure it for ourselves. But to keep the government of the moral world in his own hands, He seems to have left some cases purposely unprovided for. He has given us in this life so much good as may serve to make us thankful, but not enough to make us independent: He has caused that virtue, which generally produces happiness to those who practise it, should often fail and should sometimes make them miserable.
And now, if I have proved that there are no affections for virtue naturally implanted in man or none but what may easily be worn out; that there is no intrinsic fitness or rectitude in virtue, which can make it, in it's own nature and upon it's own account, the constant object of our choice; that neither nature nor reason will teach men steadily to pursue any thing but self-good; and that the final happiness of man does not consist merely in acting virtuously, nor can be necessarily obtained, either here or hereafter, by the sole efficacy of virtue exclusively of God and His providence;

